          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When does guidance describe antivirals as worth considering for influenza?",
          "answer": "Clinical guidance consistently recommends considering antivirals for severely ill patients with influenza. Observational evidence supports the benefit of antivirals in this group, even beyond the initial outpatient window.\n\nFor low-risk outpatients, guidance on antiviral use differs. Some authorities suggest antivirals as a reasonable option for reducing symptoms. However, other guidance and independent evidence reviews are more restrictive. These more cautious views state that the effect of antivirals on symptom duration in low-risk outpatients is modest. They also note that the effect of antivirals on complications and hospitalization in this group is not well established, and therefore, routine use is not justified.\n\nThis information comes from the \"National immunization advisory committee and infectious diseases society guidance (educational summary)\" titled \"Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment\".\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
